  "articleBody": "Schema markup isn't something you install once and forget about. It's an active ingredient in how AI systems and search engines understand your website, and keeping it fresh is key to maintaining your visibility. Just like you'd update your business information, your schema needs regular check-ups to make sure it's always accurate and effective. There's no single 'deadline' from a regulator, but smart businesses know when to revisit their structured data strategy.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\n\n*   **After a major website redesign or migration:** This is a big one! A new design often means changes to your HTML structure, content presentation, and even how your CMS generates pages. These changes can easily invalidate existing schema, leading to lost rich results. It's critical to re-validate everything.\n*   **When you introduce new services or products:** If your business evolves, your schema should too. Marking up new offerings with `Service` or `Product` schema helps AI understand what you do and can feature your new services in relevant answer engine results. Don't miss the opportunity to highlight your latest innovations.\n*   **Upon significant CMS updates or template changes:** Even minor updates to your content management system or page templates can alter how content is rendered, potentially breaking your schema. Monitoring your Search Console reports regularly will alert you to any sudden drops in rich result eligibility.\n*   **If Search Console reports show recurring schema errors:** Google's Search Console is your best friend here. If you start seeing a bunch of validation errors or warnings for your structured data, it's a clear signal that something needs attention. Ignoring these can mean missed opportunities for AI visibility.\n*   **When your content or business details change:** Did you update your business hours, phone number, address, or team members? Make sure your `LocalBusiness`, `Organization`, or `Person` schema reflects these changes. Consistency across all platforms, including your schema, builds entity trust with AI systems.\n*   **When your site has complex, dynamically generated content:** If your site pulls in content from various databases or uses advanced JavaScript to build pages, schema validation becomes trickier. This is a perfect scenario for bringing in an expert who can ensure your dynamic content is correctly marked up for AI consumption.\n\nRemember, the goal is for AI systems to have the clearest, most accurate picture of your business. By proactively maintaining your schema, you're continuously strengthening your eligibility to be cited and recommended across answer engines.",
